&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Join us on February 25&amp;nbsp;for an in-depth, student-only conversation. Registration is required, and space is limited.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Center on Global Energy Policy at Columbia University SIPA&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://womeninenergy.energypolicy.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Women in Energy initiative&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; invites you to a student roundtable with Dr. Amrita Sen, Founder and Director of Market Intelligence at Energy Aspects. This small, interactive discussion will focus on the future of energy through a company- and client-facing lens, drawing on Dr. Sen&amp;rsquo;s experience building and leading a global energy analytics firm.&lt;br /&gt;
Conversation topics will include major trends shaping the energy sector, with particular attention to power markets and energy strategy, as well as how market intelligence informs decision-making for companies, investors, and policymakers. Dr. Sen will also reflect on her career path and share practical advice on playing to your strengths, understanding what skills matter most, and knowing where to deepen expertise versus where to collaborate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Students will have the opportunity to ask questions, engage directly with an industry leader, and gain insight into careers at the intersection of energy markets, strategy, and client-facing work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This roundtable is open only to Columbia University students. Space is limited, and lunch will be served.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For more information about the event, please contact&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="mailto:energypolicyevents@columbia.edu" target="_blank"&gt;energypolicyevents@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cancer.columbia.edu/profile/maria-diaz-ordonez-md" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Maria Diaz, MD&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Assistant Professor of Neurology, Columbia University Irving Medical Center&lt;br /&gt;
Division of Neuro-Oncology (Department of Neurology)&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Program&lt;/strong&gt;: Precision Oncology and Systems Biology (POSB)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;quot;Novel diagnostic and monitoring techniques in brain tumors: Liquid biopsy, advanced imaging and beyond&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description&lt;/strong&gt;: The diagnosis, monitoring and assessment of treatment response in central nervous system tumors is plagued by multiple challenges, such as the complexity of surgical sampling of the brain and spinal cord, or the difficulty distinguishing between treatment-related changes and tumor progression in routine surveillance imaging. In this talk, we will discuss novel diagnostic and monitoring tools that aim to bypass some of these difficulties, starting with liquid biopsies in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). We will focus on the existing and potential applications of CSF liquid biopsies in routine neurooncological practice, including initial diagnosis of brain tumors, tracking tumor evolution/response to treatment, and determination of tumor progression. We will then cover advanced imaging techniques, including MRI and PET modalities, that specifically target the distinction between tumor progression and treatment-related changes, or pseudoprogression. Finally, we will discuss how a combination of multiple modalities could be integrated in future clinical practice for accurate and minimally invasive diagnosis and monitoring of brain tumors.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
